---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---
<SiteLayout
  title="When a 403 Is Good News"
  description="What Cloudflare request analytics, secret-file probes, and a custom WAF rule taught me about reading edge-security noise."
>
  <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">null route queen / security / October 2026</p>

      <h1 id="article-title" class="page-title">When a 403 Is Good News</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        Fifty-one thousand requests looked exciting until almost half of them
        turned out to be bots asking for .env files. Welcome to the public Internet.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="traffic">
      <h2 id="traffic">Request volume is not readership</h2>

      <p>
        At first glance, the traffic numbers looked impressive. Then the paths
        started telling a different story.
      </p>

      <p>
        Requests were hitting paths like <code>/.env</code>,
        <code>/.env.production</code>, <code>/backend/.env</code>,
        and <code>/config.env</code>.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is not a crowd of enthusiastic readers. That is automated scanning
        looking for secrets, credentials, configuration files, and bad deployments.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="noise">
      <h2 id="noise">Read the noise before you react to it</h2>

      <p>
        Browser, operating-system, and country charts can look surprisingly normal
        even when bots dominate the traffic. A scanner can claim to be Chrome on
        Windows just as easily as a real person can use Chrome on Windows.
      </p>

      <p>
        The useful evidence was not the headline request count. It was the combination
        of source addresses, requested paths, status codes, and Cloudflare actions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Weird traffic does not automatically mean compromise. It means: go look.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="rule">
      <h2 id="rule">Build the narrowest useful rule</h2>

      <p>
        Instead of blocking whole countries or playing whack-a-mole with individual IPs,
        I created a targeted custom rule for the behavior I actually cared about.
      </p>

      <p>
        The rule matched requests where the URI path contained <code>.env</code>
        and used the action <strong>Block</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Narrow rules are easier to reason about, easier to verify, and less likely
        to break legitimate traffic.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="verify">
      <h2 id="verify">Verify the control, do not just assume it worked</h2>

      <p>
        After deploying the rule, I tested it directly:
      </p>

      <pre><code>curl -I https://nullroutequeen.com/.env</code></pre>

      <p>The response was:</p>

      <pre><code>HTTP/2 403</code></pre>

      <p>
        Cloudflare then showed the request as a blocked event handled by <strong>Custom rules</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is the part that matters. The rule did not merely exist in the dashboard;
        it matched the request and blocked it at the edge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <h2 id="lesson">Sometimes a 403 is exactly what you wanted</h2>

      <p>
        A 403 is usually something users complain about. In this case it was proof
        that the security control worked exactly as intended.
      </p>

      <p>
        The lesson was simple: investigate the traffic, identify the behavior,
        build the narrowest useful control, and verify the result.
      </p>

      <p>
        Security gets a lot less spooky when the evidence gets to talk first.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <p><a href="/security/">← Back to Security</a></p>
</SiteLayout>
